% ============================================================
% Task 4: Abductive Hypothesis Formation
% Tests whether NARS generates a plausible explanatory
% hypothesis from an observed effect and a known rule.
%
% Inference mode: Abduction (NAL-2)
% Ground truth: abduced cause with weakened confidence
% ============================================================

% Known rule: Wet grass implies it rained
<wetGrass --> rain>. %{1.00 0.90}

% Observed effect: The grass is wet
<observation --> wetGrass>. %{1.00 0.90}

% Query: Did it rain? (abductive hypothesis)
<observation --> rain>?

% Supporting context: grass gets wet when it rains
<grass --> wetGrass>. %{0.85 0.80}

% Counter-context: sprinklers also wet grass
<sprinkler --> wetGrass>. %{0.80 0.70}

% Expected: NARS should abduct <observation --> rain> with:
%   Abduction is weaker than deduction
%   f* ~ 0.70, c* ~ 0.40
